<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harbel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harbel is a town in Margibi County, Liberia, the headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firestone-tire-and-rubber-company">Firestone</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> plantation established in 1926, one of the largest in the world. It was named after Harvey and Idabelle Firestone. Liberia's main airport, Roberts International, lies nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harbel.jpg" alt="Harbel" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fred van der Kraaij · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ASC_Leiden_-_F._van_der_Kraaij_Collection_-_14_-_37_-_A_dirt_road_near_the_Firestone_plantation._Square_houses_of_corrugated_iron_-_Harbel,_Margibi_County_(then_Marshall_County),_Liberia_-_1977-1978.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Firestone Tire and Rubber Company’s concession of 1926 created one of the world’s largest rubber plantations at <b>Harbel</b>, and rubber remained the leading export until iron ore mining began at Bomi Hills in the 1950s, followed by the Bong and Nimba mines.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liberia">Liberia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
